[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one bug, one fix queued. The reports in it held
three problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October with two outcomes, and the third problem
is still open.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but not every report in it is solved. The one-day moves have no fix: keep
their ticket open and keep sending new one-day reports to it, not to #4471.
</Callout>

## What #4471 held

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The counts are the desk's own, from the export at render time: each report once, under the
cause its tenant and way in give it. The one-day moves are counted under `parser`, where the
desk filed them, and have a ticket of their own.

## The three problems

- **`parser`**, fixed. The shared parser read every date month first, so a day-first tenant's
  date came out with day and month swapped (7 April became 4 July). Engineering shipped
  `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- **`hint`**, won't fix. The booking-v3 form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and read
  the date day first. Same swap, but only through the v3 form, so the parser fix never touched
  it. The booking-v3 form is retired, so nothing new comes in this way.
- **`one-day`**, open. No swap at all: the date moves by one day (the invite says Tuesday 12 May,
  the calendar Monday 11 May), two clients in different time zones get different days for the
  same meeting, or an invite arrives before the booking was made. That points at time zones,
  not date order. Neither outcome covers it.

Which problem a report belongs to depends on its tenant, its way in and what it describes. The
intake tag does not decide it: it is a guess from the reporter's browser.

## Why they were merged

All three came in under the same subject lines ("Date is wrong", even "Month and day swapped?"
on a report where nothing was swapped). [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
merges on subject alone, so the subject never told them apart. Only the tenant, the way in and
the body do.

## Lessons

- A big ticket can hold more than one bug. Before marking it known, group it by what a fix
  would have to change, not by subject.
- Closing the big ticket is not closing its reports. Check each problem against what shipped.

